{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-56557",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Since we are given that ${a}_{3}=8$, we can use the formula for the $n$th term to solve for $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ 8 & =2\\cdot {r}^{3-1} \\\\ 4 & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {2}^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are given that the sequence is positive (meaning all its terms must be positive numbers), the common ratio must be positive, and we discard the negative solution. Therefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =2\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={2}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives ${a}_{n}=48,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}48 & ={2}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nHowever, $48$ is not a power of $2,$ so there is no integer solution to the equation above. Therefore, $48$ is not a term in the geometric sequence.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
